# Prefer extending existing tests

When adding coverage for a change, extend an existing related test first. Do not create a large new test file or duplicate setup unless nothing suitable exists.

Do not remove or replace existing `test(...)` / `it(...)` cases when adding new ones. Keep them unless the behavior is gone or the test is wrong, and only then with a clear reason.

## Order of preference

1. Add or adjust a case in an existing test that already loads the same packages, DB, fixtures, or feature area.
2. Reuse shared helpers and fixtures (`test/test-util.js`, existing `test/resource/**`, patterns in nearby `*.test.js`).
3. Only add a new `*.test.js` when the behavior is a distinct area with no reasonable host test.

## Avoid

- Copy-pasting `beforeAll` DB/schema/ZCL/template load blocks into a new file when an existing suite already has that context.
- New fixtures that duplicate `test/resource/meta`, `test/gen-template/**`, or similar.
- Large standalone suites whose only purpose is one small assertion that fits an existing test.

```javascript
// ❌ BAD — new file that re-boots DB + templates just for one mapping check
// test/my-new-component-mapping.test.js with full beforeAll setup...

// ✅ GOOD — extend the suite that already loads templates / IDE integration
test('Multiple clusters can map to the same UC component', async () => {
  // additional assertions in test/ide-integration.test.js
})

// ❌ BAD — drop an existing case to add a new one
// removed: it('Should have delete button for notifications', ...)
it('Should have a resizable notifications drawer', () => { ... })

// ✅ GOOD — keep existing cases; append new ones
it('Should have delete button for notifications', () => { ... })
it('Should have a resizable notifications drawer', () => { ... })
```

Keep new test code minimal: only what proves the new behavior. Match the style and timeout helpers of the host file.
